= Model selection bias =

== Overview ==
'''Model selection bias''' occurs when a model, hyperparameter setting, feature set, or other configuration is chosen because it performs best on a noisy finite-sample selection criterion, causing the selected score to overestimate future performance.

It is a form of adaptive overfitting to the model-selection process.

== Selection over many candidates ==
Suppose many candidate systems have similar true performance but noisy validation estimates.

Selecting the highest observed score tends to select both genuine quality and favorable noise.

The selected validation score is therefore optimistic on average.

== Validation overfitting ==
The effect becomes stronger when:

* many configurations are tried;
* validation data are small or noisy;
* researchers repeatedly inspect results;
* the search process adapts to prior validation outcomes;
* benchmark leaderboards are used as development feedback.

== Avoidance ==
Approaches include:

* independent final testing;
* nested cross-validation;
* fresh validation data;
* correction for multiple/adaptive comparisons;
* prespecified model-selection procedures;
* reporting uncertainty and search scope.

== Relation to selection bias ==
[[Selection bias]] in statistics concerns how observations enter a sample or analysis.

Model selection bias concerns choosing among candidate models/configurations based on noisy performance estimates.

The terms are related only at a broad statistical level.

== Related concepts ==
[[Validation set]], [[Test set]], [[Overfitting]], [[Selection bias]], [[Benchmark contamination]], and [[Generalization (machine learning)]] distinguish the main evaluation failures.

